@page "/documentation"

<PageTitle>Documentation | LMS HA Edge Gateway</PageTitle>

<div class="page-shell edge-gateway-page docs-page">
    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-hero">
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
            <h2>LMS HA Edge Gateway Handbook</h2>
            <p class="meta-text">
                A complete operating guide for publishing Home Assistant and any internal web app through Cloudflare Tunnel,
                Caddy, and LMS authentication without opening inbound firewall ports.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-hero-flow" aria-label="Traffic flow">
            <span>Browser</span>
            <span>Cloudflare</span>
            <span>cloudflared</span>
            <span>Caddy</span>
            <span>Internal app</span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section docs-quick-map">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Read this first</p>
                <h2>What Edge Gateway is doing for you</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Edge Gateway turns each published hostname into a managed route. Cloudflare handles the public edge and
                    tunnel transport, cloudflared keeps an outbound connector alive from this add-on, Caddy routes traffic to
                    the correct LAN service, and LMS applies the selected access policy before the upstream app is reached.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-flow-list">
            <div><strong>1. DNS</strong><span>A public hostname such as <code>plex.example.com</code> points at the managed Edge Gateway relay namespace.</span></div>
            <div><strong>2. Tunnel</strong><span>Cloudflare routes the request down the named tunnel to the local cloudflared process in the add-on.</span></div>
            <div><strong>3. Caddy</strong><span>Caddy matches the public Host header and optional path prefix, then proxies to the configured internal target.</span></div>
            <div><strong>4. Auth</strong><span>For protected apps, Caddy asks LMS forward-auth whether to allow, redirect to login, deny, or pass through.</span></div>
        </div>

        <p class="callout ready">
            For browser-based internal apps this gives a VPN-grade access pattern with less friction: no inbound ports, no full
            network tunnel, no VPN client rollout, and per-app MFA/passkey controls. It is not a replacement for every VPN use case,
            but it is a better fit when the thing you want to expose is a web UI.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Setup path</p>
                <h2>Before you publish the first app</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">Have these in place before relying on external access.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-check-grid">
            <article>
                <strong>Cloudflare account</strong>
                <span>The add-on uses Cloudflare APIs to create or reuse a tunnel, write DNS records, and keep ingress aligned.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Cloudflare-managed domain</strong>
                <span>Your domain must use Cloudflare DNS. Edge Gateway can only manage zones returned by the saved token.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Scoped API token</strong>
                <span>Use a custom token with only the required account and zone permissions for the domains this add-on should manage.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Email provider</strong>
                <span>Recommended. It enables setup emails and email OTP fallback. Supported providers include SMTP, Microsoft Graph, Resend, Brevo, MailerSend, and Mailgun; many offer free tiers.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Internal targets</strong>
                <span>Know the scheme, host/IP, port, and any path prefix for each app you want to publish.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Mail Relay (optional)</strong>
                <span>Outbound SMTP for LAN apps, with Cloudflare SPF/DKIM/DMARC and automatic public-IP DNS refresh. Separate from Security → Messaging.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Admin account</strong>
                <span>Create at least one LMS user, enroll authenticator OTP, then save a passkey from the public HTTPS route.</span>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="cloudflare-token">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Cloudflare token</p>
                <h2>Create and save the scoped token</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Go to Cloudflare API Tokens, create a custom token, scope it to the account and zone this add-on should manage,
                    then paste it into Setup. The token is stored under the add-on data root.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-two-column">
            <div>
                <h3>Required permission rows</h3>
                <ul class="plain-list">
                    <li><code>Account</code> / <code>Cloudflare Tunnel</code> / <code>Edit</code></li>
                    <li><code>Account</code> / <code>Access: Apps and Policies</code> / <code>Edit</code></li>
                    <li><code>Zone</code> / <code>DNS</code> / <code>Edit</code></li>
                </ul>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Keep the token narrow. Prefer one account and only the zones this add-on is allowed to publish.
                    Rotate the token if it is copied outside Home Assistant or if the add-on host is decommissioned.
                </p>
            </div>

            <figure class="docs-screenshot-card">
                <img src="images/cloudflare-api-token-permissions.png" alt="Cloudflare API token permissions for Cloudflare Tunnel, Access Apps and Policies, and DNS edit access." />
                <figcaption>
                    <strong>Cloudflare API token permissions</strong>
                    <small>Account tunnel edit, account Access app/policy edit, and zone DNS edit.</small>
                </figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="relay">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Domain and relay</p>
                <h2>How the wildcard relay namespace works</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Setup Relay prepares the Cloudflare side once for a domain. Published apps then reuse that managed relay
                    namespace instead of each app needing hand-built tunnel and DNS plumbing.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-route-diagram">
            <div><strong>Relay namespace</strong><code>ha-app-relay.example.com</code></div>
            <div><strong>Wildcard DNS</strong><code>*.ha-app-relay.example.com -> tunnel-id.cfargotunnel.com</code></div>
            <div><strong>App DNS</strong><code>plex.example.com -> ha-app-relay.example.com</code></div>
            <div><strong>Caddy target</strong><code>http://192.168.15.40:32400</code></div>
        </div>

        <h3>What Setup Relay creates</h3>
        <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li>Finds the selected Cloudflare zone and account from the saved token.</li>
            <li>Creates or reuses a named Cloudflare Tunnel for the relay namespace.</li>
            <li>Creates the proxied wildcard DNS record for <code>*.ha-app-relay.example.com</code>.</li>
            <li>Saves the connector token used by cloudflared inside the add-on.</li>
            <li>Writes tunnel ingress so Cloudflare sends matching hostnames to local Caddy.</li>
            <li>Applies the Caddyfile so host/path matches route to the right internal service.</li>
        </ol>

        <figure class="docs-screenshot-card docs-screenshot-row">
            <img src="images/docs-published-apps-add-route.png" alt="Setup Relay showing a healthy local relay for linuxmadesane.com with the Add action available." />
            <figcaption>
                <strong>Setup Relay ready</strong>
                <small>The domain relay is configured, the tunnel is healthy, and apps can now be added for the zone.</small>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p class="callout ready">
            Public TLS is handled at Cloudflare. Caddy then controls the internal hop to your service, including Host headers,
            forwarded headers, path prefix handling, and whether an HTTPS upstream with a self-signed or IP-address certificate
            should skip upstream TLS verification.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="publishing">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Publishing apps</p>
                <h2>Expose any internal web app securely</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    If a service has a web interface on your LAN, Edge Gateway can usually publish it. Home Assistant, Plex,
                    Jellyfin, Proxmox, ESXi, routers, NAS tools, dashboards, admin consoles, and custom web apps all follow the
                    same route model.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-two-column">
            <div>
                <h3>Add app fields</h3>
                <div class="docs-definition-list">
                    <div><strong>App name</strong><span>Human-readable label in Published Apps.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Public host</strong><span>The external hostname users will open, for example <code>plex.example.com</code>.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Target scheme/host/port</strong><span>The internal service, for example <code>http://192.168.15.40:32400</code>.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Path prefix</strong><span>Optional. Caddy strips this before proxying when you publish under a path.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Access policy</strong><span>MFA/Passkey, Email approve IP, or Pass Through. Disable the route when it should not be reachable.</span></div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="docs-screenshot-card docs-screenshot-row">
            <img src="images/docs-published-apps-added-service.png" alt="Published Apps showing the Home Assistant service after it has been added, including external and internal addresses." />
            <figcaption>
                <strong>Adding a service</strong>
                <small>The saved route shows policy state plus the external hostname and internal target.</small>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Service discovery</h3>
        <p class="meta-text">
            Discovery is deliberately targeted. It starts from what the host already knows, then probes likely HTTP services instead
            of blindly walking every address and every port.
        </p>
        <div class="docs-pipeline">
            <span>ARP / neighbour cache</span>
            <span>SSDP</span>
            <span>mDNS</span>
            <span>WS-Discovery</span>
            <span>Docker / Supervisor</span>
            <span>Known ports</span>
            <span>Targeted probes</span>
        </div>
        <p class="meta-text">
            In Home Assistant, host network visibility can differ from a normal Linux shell. Expanded LAN discovery asks Home
            Assistant and the host network for broader CIDR details where available, then folds those candidates into the same
            targeted probe pipeline.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="domain-services">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Domain Services</p>
                <h2>Publish .well-known services</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Domain Services manages public files and JSON metadata under <code>/.well-known/</code> for domains already
                    routed through Edge Gateway. This covers verification files, public keys, app links, security contacts,
                    WebFinger, and OAuth/OIDC discovery without manually editing Caddy or Linux paths.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-check-grid">
            <article>
                <strong>Tesla Fleet</strong>
                <span>Generates an EC key pair, stores the private key under add-on secrets, and publishes only the public key at Tesla's required path.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>security.txt</strong>
                <span>Builds a valid text file with contact, expiry, preferred languages, canonical, policy, and hiring links.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>App and identity metadata</strong>
                <span>Templates cover WebFinger, Apple App Site Association, Android Asset Links, OpenID/OAuth discovery, custom text, and custom JSON.</span>
            </article>
        </div>

        <p class="callout ready">
            .well-known routes are public by default and generated before normal app routes, so they bypass LMS forward-auth unless
            you explicitly require auth. The manager validates paths, prevents traversal, writes files under
            <code>/share/lms-edge-gateway/well-known/public</code>, refreshes Caddy safely, and verifies the public URL over HTTPS.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="authentication">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Authentication options</p>
                <h2>Choose the right access policy per app</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Every published route has its own policy. Use the strictest policy that still fits the app and client.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-policy-grid">
            <article>
                <strong>MFA/Passkey</strong>
                <span>Best default for browser-first apps. Users authenticate with LMS, satisfy MFA, then Caddy forwards the request.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Email approve IP</strong>
                <span>Useful for clients that cannot complete browser MFA. Edge Gateway denies the first request, emails an approval link, then temporarily allows only that app from the exact source IP.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Pass Through</strong>
                <span>Use only when the upstream app is public by design, has its own authentication, or native clients cannot handle the LMS browser flow. Internet traffic is proxied straight to the destination service.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Source restrictions</strong>
                <span>Layer known IPs, LAN-only rules, allowed users, or allowed groups on top of the selected route policy.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Disabled routes</strong>
                <span>Turn a route off when you want to keep the configuration but stop external access.</span>
            </article>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-two-column">
            <div>
                <h3>Email approve IP flow</h3>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Email approve IP is designed for apps such as Plex or other native clients where a browser redirect to LMS MFA
                    would break the client. It is still deny-first: the first external request is blocked, an email is sent to
                    configured LMS users, and access only opens after a user deliberately approves that specific source IP.
                    By default the grant is also bound to the requesting client User-Agent, so other apps on the same public IP stay blocked.
                </p>
                <ol class="numbered-list">
                    <li>A client requests the published hostname, for example <code>plex.example.com</code>.</li>
                    <li>Caddy asks LMS forward-auth to check the route. Because the source IP is not approved yet, the request is denied.</li>
                    <li>Edge Gateway sends a throttled approval email with the app name, requested URL, source IP, approximate Cloudflare country, and user agent.</li>
                    <li>The recipient clicks the approval link from email. The link creates a temporary grant for that route and exact source IP. By default it also requires the same client User-Agent.</li>
                    <li>The original client retries from the same public IP. Edge Gateway allows it until the idle cut-off or maximum expiry is reached.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Approval grants are not reusable across apps or hostnames. Approving from Chrome still works for VLC or Plex on the
                    same device because the grant is created from the original client request, not from the browser that clicked Allow.
                    If the client's public IP changes, the new IP has to be approved separately. Repeated emails for the same app/client
                    are throttled so media clients cannot flood the mailbox while waiting for approval.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>Email approve IP settings</h3>
                <div class="docs-definition-list compact">
                    <div><strong>Approval recipients</strong><span>Select enabled LMS users. Blank falls back to route allowed users, then enabled LMS users.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Allowed countries</strong><span>Optional Cloudflare country allow-list. Requests outside the selected countries are denied before email is sent.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Denied response</strong><span>Leave Return 404 off to show the access check page for all unapproved clients (production included). Enable Return 404 only when you want a silent Not Found instead.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Shared IP access</strong><span>Off by default. When enabled, any client on the approved public IP may use the route. Use this for trusted home or LAN public IPs.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Verified LAN trust</strong><span>Optional. Skip MFA/Email approve IP when the real LAN source IP is inside your trusted CIDRs and reverse DNS (plus forward confirm) matches your internal domain. Cloudflare internet clients are never trusted this way.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Idle cut-off</strong><span>How long the approved IP remains open after the last seen request. Blank uses the add-on default.</span></div>
                    <div><strong>Maximum expiry</strong><span>Absolute lifetime for the approval grant even if traffic continues. Blank uses the add-on default.</span></div>
                </div>
                <p class="callout ready">
                    Email approve IP is not the same as pass-through. Pass-through sends traffic straight to the upstream service.
                    Email approve IP keeps Edge Gateway in front, denies by default, requires explicit email approval, and then opens
                    a temporary route for one source IP and, by default, the requesting client only.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-two-column">
            <div>
                <h3>Why passkeys are the best option</h3>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Passkeys are phishing-resistant and bound to the public HTTPS origin, so a copied password or OTP is not enough.
                    The browser or device proves possession of the private key and usually requires the user to unlock the device.
                    There is no shared secret to type, no code to relay, and no reusable password for an attacker to steal.
                </p>
                <ol class="numbered-list">
                    <li>Create or reset an LMS user so they have an authenticator OTP QR code.</li>
                    <li>Sign in through the public Edge Gateway route with OTP or email OTP.</li>
                    <li>Use the save passkey prompt to register this device with the current public origin.</li>
                    <li>Next time, choose Passkey and approve the device prompt instead of typing a code.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Save passkeys from the real external HTTPS hostname. WebAuthn checks the origin, so registering from a local
                    HTTP address and using it from the public HTTPS route will fail by design.
                </p>
            </div>
            <figure class="docs-screenshot-card">
                <img src="images/docs-login-passkey-flow.png" alt="LMS Edge Gateway login screen showing MFA/passkey required, OTP sign-in, email code option, and passkey setup checkbox." />
                <figcaption>
                    <strong>Login and passkey setup flow</strong>
                    <small>OTP sign-in, email fallback, passkey login, and the option to save a passkey after MFA.</small>
                </figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="messaging">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Messaging</p>
                <h2>Set up email early</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Email is not mandatory for local testing, but it makes account provisioning and recovery far smoother.
                    A successful test email marks the selected provider as verified and enables email for LMS account setup workflows.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-definition-list compact">
            <div><strong>SMTP</strong><span>Works with a standard SMTP server or relay account.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Microsoft Graph</strong><span>Uses an Entra app registration and a mailbox allowed to send setup messages.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Resend, Brevo, MailerSend, Mailgun</strong><span>API-based transactional providers. Many have free tiers; check current provider limits before choosing one.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="callout ready">
            Recommended flow: choose a provider, save the settings, send a successful test to yourself, then create LMS users.
            That gives each user a cleaner setup path and gives you email OTP as a fallback when a passkey is not yet enrolled.
        </p>
        <p class="callout">
            Messaging is an <strong>outbound client</strong> used by Edge Gateway itself (account setup, email OTP, IP approval).
            Mail Relay is a separate <strong>outbound SMTP server</strong> on private TCP/587 for applications on your LAN.
            Do not point Messaging at a public smart-host unless you intend Edge Gateway to send through that provider.
            After Mail Relay is running, you can optionally point Messaging SMTP at <code>127.0.0.1:587</code> using a Mail Relay username.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="mail-relay">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Mail Relay</p>
                <h2>Outbound SMTP from a home IP</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">
                    Mail Relay is outbound-only. It is not an inbound mailbox or MX. Postfix, OpenDKIM and SASL run inside this add-on.
                    Public TCP/587 is never opened. Authenticated STARTTLS on private 587 is the default; restricted legacy port 25 is optional.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-definition-list compact">
            <div><strong>Cloudflare DNS</strong><span>Creates or merges unproxied A for <code>smtp.yourzone</code>, SPF <code>ip4:</code> for this WAN, LMS DKIM, and DMARC <code>p=none</code> only if missing. Existing MX and other people's DKIM/DMARC are left alone.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Public IP refresh</strong><span>Home WAN addresses change. Mail Relay checks Cloudflare's <code>cdn-cgi/trace</code> every 15 minutes by default and rewrites only LMS-owned A and SPF <code>ip4:</code> values.</span></div>
            <div><strong>PTR</strong><span>Reverse DNS stays with your ISP. A mismatch is a warning, not a blocker.</span></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="scenarios">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Examples</p>
                <h2>Practical publishing scenarios</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">Use these as starting points for real routes.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-scenario-list">
            <article>
                <strong>Home Assistant from outside the house</strong>
                <span>Publish <code>hassio.example.com</code> to the internal Home Assistant URL. Use pass-through if the HA mobile app or native HA auth needs to own the login flow; use MFA/Passkey for browser admin access.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Plex or Jellyfin for family access</strong>
                <span>Publish the media UI without opening router ports. Email approve IP can fit native media clients because it avoids browser MFA while still requiring deliberate approval for each source IP. Pass-through can also fit when the upstream app's own auth is strong enough.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Admin tools such as routers, NAS, Proxmox, or ESXi</strong>
                <span>Use MFA/Passkey, known IPs, and user/group restrictions. These are high-impact apps, so avoid pass-through unless the upstream auth is strong.</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Temporary troubleshooting access</strong>
                <span>Create a route, leave it disabled until needed, enable it during a support window, then disable or delete it afterwards. Diagnostics and route tests confirm whether Cloudflare, Caddy, and the upstream target are healthy.</span>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface-card edge-card docs-section" id="operations">
        <div class="table-header">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Operations</p>
                <h2>Repair, test, and keep it healthy</h2>
                <p class="meta-text">The app is designed to make the moving parts visible without making you hand-edit them.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-definition-list">
            <div><strong>Setup tab</strong><span>Validates the token, relay zones, tunnel status, wildcard DNS, connector token, cloudflared, and Caddy readiness.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Published Apps</strong><span>Shows each domain group, lets you add, edit, enable, disable, test, or delete routes.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Diagnostics</strong><span>Shows route checks, access events, and operational signals for why a route is allowed, denied, or failing.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Repair behavior</strong><span>Startup and Setup validation reconcile tunnel ingress, DNS, Caddy, and cloudflared where possible. If Cloudflare reports the tunnel down, the add-on tries to restart or directly start cloudflared before asking you to rebuild anything.</span></div>
        </div>

        <h3>Home Assistant install progress</h3>
        <p class="meta-text">
            Home Assistant can jump from <code>0%</code> to <code>100%</code> and still show <code>Installing</code>. That is
            Supervisor reporting a coarse install phase, not proof that the Edge Gateway app is ready yet.
        </p>
        <p class="meta-text">
            This add-on is currently built locally by Home Assistant from the repository Dockerfile. The HA host still has to
            restore and publish the .NET app, install Caddy, download cloudflared, create the Docker image, and start the
            supervised services. On smaller Home Assistant hardware that can take long enough to look stuck.
        </p>
        <p class="callout ready">
            Prebuilt multi-architecture registry images would remove the long local build step. Until then, treat the Supervisor
            progress bar as a broad Docker build/install indicator, then use Setup validation after startup to confirm token,
            tunnel, DNS, cloudflared, Caddy, and route health.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
